Optimal Solution - I keep the smaller half in a max-heap and the larger half in a min-heap, balancing their sizes so the median is always accessible in constant time.

class MedianFinder {
    // Two PriorityQueues to maintain the elements in max and min heaps.
    PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap; // Max heap for the smaller half of the numbers.
    PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap; // Min heap for the larger half of the numbers.
    public MedianFinder() {
        // Constructor: Initialize the two heaps with custom comparators.
        // In maxHeap, the larger numbers are at the root.
        // In minHeap, the smaller numbers are at the root.
        maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<Integer>((a, b) -> b - a);
        minHeap = new PriorityQueue<Integer>((a, b) -> a - b);
    }
    public void addNum(int num) {
        // Method to add a number to the median finder.
        if (maxHeap.isEmpty() || maxHeap.peek() >= num)
            maxHeap.add(num); // If the number is smaller or equal to the maximum in the max heap, add it to maxHeap.
        else
            minHeap.add(num); // Otherwise, add it to minHeap.
        // Ensure the balance between maxHeap and minHeap. If one is larger by more than 1 element, rebalance.
        if (maxHeap.size() > minHeap.size() + 1)
            minHeap.add(maxHeap.poll());
        else if (maxHeap.size() < minHeap.size())
            maxHeap.add(minHeap.poll());
    }
    public double findMedian() {
        // Method to find the median of the numbers inserted so far.
        if (maxHeap.size() == minHeap.size()) {
            // If both heaps have the same number of elements, the median is the average of their roots.
            return (maxHeap.peek() + minHeap.peek()) / 2.0;
        } else {
            // Otherwise, the median is the root of the maxHeap since it contains the middle element.
            return maxHeap.peek();
        }
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(log n).
Space Complexity :- O(n).
